{"id": "2026-10-19-news-macron-keeps-interior-minister-after-riots", "date": "2026-10-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Macron keeps interior minister after riots", "summary": "Macron refused Interior Minister Nuñez's resignation after weekend riots; arrests passed 2,300 and French bond yields rose.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/19/frances-macron-declines-ministers-resignation-after-violent-protests", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "French Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez offered to resign over the weekend's riots, and President Emmanuel Macron turned him down on Monday 19 October. In a statement, the Élysée said Nuñez must stay to keep the chain of command in the police intact. Macron also asked him to find new ways to deal with looters and to \"take all necessary steps to ensure transparency over the failures in planning and intelligence that led to the violence\". Nuñez admitted that police \"lost control\" on Saturday 17 October and that there had been a \"lack of anticipation\", even with about 60,000 officers on duty. The interior ministry now says more than 2,300 people were arrested and almost 1,000 police and gendarmes were injured on Saturday, up from the 1,800 arrests and 500 injuries it gave on Sunday. Looting went on in Paris on Sunday 18 October, and Marseille banned gatherings from 12:00 to 20:00 on Monday. Markets reacted: France's 10-year borrowing cost rose 0.10 percentage points to 4.885%, widening its gap with Germany's, at a time when the government is trying to pass a budget that cuts spending. Switzerland's foreign ministry (FDFA) told Swiss travellers in France to stay away from demonstrations and follow the authorities' instructions.", "why": "France's debt was 115.6% of its economic output in 2025, so rising borrowing costs make the budget harder to pass six months before the presidential election, and the protests show no sign of ending.", "thread_id": "2026-10-19-news-macron-keeps-interior-minister-after-riots", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-19", "event_date": "2026-10-19", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["France", "Emmanuel Macron", "Laurent Nuñez", "Marseille"]}
{"id": "2026-10-19-news-russian-missiles-black-out-550-000-ukrainian-hom", "date": "2026-10-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Russian missiles black out 550,000 Ukrainian homes", "summary": "Russia's 16-missile strike on Ukraine's grid cut power to 550,000 homes a day after Ukraine's 1,015-drone attack on Russia; Rubio denied an intelligence pause.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/ukraine-got-no-warning-of-russian-ballistic-strikes-zelenskyy-says/a-79697476", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Russia fired 16 ballistic missiles at Ukraine's energy system overnight into Monday 19 October, cutting power to about 550,000 households, a day after Ukraine launched one of its largest drone attacks of the war on Russia. President Volodymyr Zelensky said 10 of the missiles hit their targets, and that once again \"there was no early warning from our partners\". Speaking in Uzbekistan, US Secretary of State Marco Rubio denied Zelensky's claim on Sunday that US intelligence sharing had been \"blocked for some period of time\": \"There's been no pause.\" On Sunday 18 October, Russia's defence ministry said it had downed 1,015 Ukrainian drones over 15 regions. The St Petersburg governor said strikes on the city's oil terminal and a fuel depot started fires, killed one person and injured five. On Sunday, President Donald Trump defended pausing new Russia sanctions, saying he did not want to \"foolishly\" drive Moscow \"away from the bargaining table\", and he claimed that Zelensky \"doesn't want to end this war\". He left out that, according to Zelensky, Russia hit Ukraine's energy sector 76 times in the week after Trump announced an \"energy ceasefire\" on 11 October.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-19-news-russian-missiles-black-out-550-000-ukrainian-hom", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-19", "event_date": "2026-10-19", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Ukraine", "Russia", "Volodymyr Zelensky", "Marco Rubio", "St Petersburg"]}
{"id": "2026-10-19-news-wfp-cuts-gaza-food-aid-by-a-third", "date": "2026-10-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "WFP cuts Gaza food aid by a third", "summary": "WFP said funding shortfalls forced it to cut Gaza food aid from 1.6 to 1.1 million people a month.", "url": "https://www.wfp.org/news/funding-cuts-force-wfp-drastically-reduce-lifesaving-assistance-gaza", "source_domain": "wfp.org", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "health"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The UN World Food Programme said on Monday 19 October that a lack of funding has forced it to cut food aid in Gaza by about a third, so half a million people will lose help. Since this month WFP has been feeding 1.1 million people, down from 1.6 million in September. Food parcels have been halved, hot meals cut from 250,000 to 150,000 a day, and people near the \"yellow line\" of Israeli military control now get a hot meal only every other day. About 1.4 million people, 67% of Gaza's population, face acute food insecurity, and 200,000 of them are in \"Emergency\" conditions (IPC Phase 4 on the UN-backed hunger scale, one step below famine). More than 80% of people are unemployed. WFP says it has received less than half its usual funding for Palestine in 2026 and needs USD 450 million to keep critical operations going in Gaza and the West Bank until March 2027.", "why": "a year after the October 2025 ceasefire, reconstruction has not started and most families still depend on aid, so the improvements since then could quickly be reversed as winter comes.", "thread_id": "2026-10-19-news-wfp-cuts-gaza-food-aid-by-a-third", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-19", "event_date": "2026-10-19", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["World Food Programme", "Gaza", "West Bank"]}
{"id": "2026-10-19-news-milan-prosecutors-seek-jail-for-11-rioters", "date": "2026-10-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Milan prosecutors seek jail for 11 rioters", "summary": "After two days of cost-of-living riots in Milan, prosecutors sought custody for 11 and Meloni convened a security meeting.", "url": "https://www.ansa.it/sito/notizie/cronaca/2026/10/19/il-movimento-17-ottobre-annuncia-mobilitazione-permanente-meloni-convoca-un-vertice_0d0d286f-7f8f-420a-9737-2566da0ba8a2.html", "source_domain": "ansa.it", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Milan prosecutors are seeking jail custody for 11 young people arrested in two days of rioting, and Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni called a meeting on Monday 19 October to discuss \"new tools\" against \"those who sow chaos\". Police sources told ANSA that 14 officers were injured in Milan on Sunday 18 October and 271 people were taken to police headquarters, 220 of them minors; 32 were arrested. The 11 accused, most born in 2007–2008, face charges including \"devastation and looting\", which carries 8 to 15 years in prison. A new online group, \"Movimento 17 ottobre\", has called for protests every Saturday \"until the fall of this government\". The Italian unrest grew out of the same cost-of-living protests that spread from France on Saturday 17 October.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-19-news-milan-prosecutors-seek-jail-for-11-rioters", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-19", "event_date": "2026-10-18", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Italy", "Giorgia Meloni", "Milan"]}
{"id": "2026-10-19-news-china-s-growth-reaches-4-5-on-exports", "date": "2026-10-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "China's growth reaches 4.5% on exports", "summary": "China's NBS said Q3 GDP grew 4.5% year on year, with exports strong and retail sales weak.", "url": "https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202610/t20261019_1965619.html", "source_domain": "stats.gov.cn", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "China's economy grew 4.5% year on year from July to September, a little faster than the 4.3% of the second quarter, the National Bureau of Statistics said on Monday 19 October. Growth over the first nine months was 4.6%. Exports drove it: they rose 16.4% in yuan terms over nine months and 29.6% in September alone, led by machinery and electronics. Spending at home stayed weak, with retail sales of consumer goods up only 1.1% in September and over the nine months.", "why": "China's growth depends on selling abroad while its own consumers spend little, which leaves it exposed to trade disputes with its partners.", "thread_id": "2026-10-19-news-china-s-growth-reaches-4-5-on-exports", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-19", "event_date": "2026-10-19", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["China", "National Bureau of Statistics of China"]}
{"id": "2026-10-19-news-australian-mp-helen-haines-hit-by-car", "date": "2026-10-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Australian MP Helen Haines hit by car", "summary": "Independent MP Helen Haines was critically injured by an SUV outside her Wangaratta office; a 43-year-old man was arrested.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/australian-mp-seriously-hurt-in-suspected-car-attack/a-79697660", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Australian independent MP Helen Haines was critically injured on Monday 19 October when an SUV hit her outside her office in Wangaratta, Victoria, and counter-terrorism investigators have joined the police inquiry. Prime Minister Anthony Albanese said a 43-year-old man had been arrested: \"This wasn't an accident. There was a deliberate act, it would appear.\" He said it was too early to talk about a motive. Haines, 65, has sat in parliament since 2019. Threats and harassment against politicians in Australia have risen in recent years.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-19-news-australian-mp-helen-haines-hit-by-car", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-19", "event_date": "2026-10-19", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Helen Haines", "Australia", "Anthony Albanese"]}
{"id": "2026-10-19-news-canada-base-fire-toll-confirmed-at-four", "date": "2026-10-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Canada base fire toll confirmed at four", "summary": "Four soldiers died and 19 are critical after a wind-driven grass fire at CFB Wainwright during training.", "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/18/serious-incident-alberta-military-base-deaths-injuries-canada-carney-cfb-wainwright", "source_domain": "theguardian.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["world"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Four soldiers were killed in Saturday's fire at Canadian Forces Base Wainwright, not the seven reported earlier by unnamed sources, and 39 people are in hospital, 19 of them in critical condition. The army named the dead as Sgt Morgan Wong and Privates Shaun Farrell, Aidan Goulding and Hayden Lykkemark. The commander of the 1st Canadian Division said a grass fire started during combat training on Saturday 17 October. Officials thought it was under control until winds of up to 96 km/h turned it towards the soldiers: \"The fire was a kilometre away when the wind shifted.\" The cause is under investigation. Prime Minister Mark Carney called it one of the worst tragedies for the forces on Canadian soil in peacetime.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-19-news-canada-base-fire-toll-confirmed-at-four", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-19", "event_date": "2026-10-17", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Canada", "CFB Wainwright", "Mark Carney"]}
{"id": "2026-10-19-news-securitas-logged-20-000-labour-law-breaches", "date": "2026-10-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Securitas logged 20,000 labour-law breaches", "summary": "SRF found internal Securitas data recording over 20,000 labour-law breaches in central Switzerland in 2023-2025.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/missstand-bei-sicherheitsfirma-so-systematisch-beutet-securitas-ihre-mitarbeitenden-aus", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Internal Securitas data show more than 20,000 breaches of Swiss labour law at its central Switzerland branch from 2023 to 2025, affecting more than 900 staff, SRF reported on Monday 19 October. SRF's investigative and data teams checked records from the security company's Lucerne regional office. The records show night shifts longer than the legal maximum of 13 hours, rest periods of only a few hours and weeks above the legal limit of 50 hours. One shift can count as several breaches, and Securitas says it cannot match the 20,000 figure. Staff at federal asylum centres were affected too: at Glaubenberg (Obwalden), about 80 employees worked at least 1,800 hours more than allowed. Securitas admits its practice \"partly\" does not follow labour-law principles but denies breaking the law systematically, saying the overtime is recorded and paid. The State Secretariat for Migration (SEM) has started checks. The labour inspectorates of the central Swiss cantons say they did not know about the breaches and will look into them.", "why": "companies bidding for public contracts only have to declare themselves that they follow labour law, and two labour-law experts quoted by SRF say the authorities have to step in.", "thread_id": "2026-10-19-news-securitas-logged-20-000-labour-law-breaches", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-19", "event_date": "2026-10-19", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Securitas", "SEM", "Central Switzerland"]}
{"id": "2026-10-19-news-lindt-ousts-ceo-adalbert-lechner", "date": "2026-10-19", "stream": "news", "headline": "Lindt ousts CEO Adalbert Lechner", "summary": "Lindt & Sprüngli removed CEO Adalbert Lechner with immediate effect; Dieter Weisskopf is interim CEO.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/lindt-sprungli-remercie-son-directeur-general-adalbert-lechner", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Lindt & Sprüngli removed its chief executive Adalbert Lechner with immediate effect on Monday 19 October, after cutting its sales forecast twice this year. Former CEO Dieter Weisskopf, who ran the company from 2016 to 2022 and is now vice-chairman of the board, takes over for now; the Kilchberg (ZH) chocolate maker hopes to name a permanent successor in the second quarter of 2027. The board said it needs new leadership to return the group to \"robust, sustained growth\". Lechner, 65, leaves after 33 years with the company. Lindt raised its prices sharply when cocoa prices jumped, then lowered some of them again in the summer to win back customers as sales volumes fell. It now expects organic sales growth of at most 2% this year.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-19-news-lindt-ousts-ceo-adalbert-lechner", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-19", "event_date": "2026-10-19", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Lindt & Sprüngli", "Adalbert Lechner", "Dieter Weisskopf"]}
